Spring/Spring Boot
==================
#What is Inversion of Control (IoC)?
-------------------------------------
Definition:
IoC means the control of creating and managing objects is transferred from the application to the Spring Container.

Without Spring, you create objects yourself.

Without IoC
class EmployeeService {

    EmployeeRepository repository = new EmployeeRepository();

    public void saveEmployee() {
        repository.save();
    }
}

Here:

EmployeeService creates EmployeeRepository.
The class is tightly coupled.
With IoC
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    public void saveEmployee() {
        repository.save();
    }
}

Here:

Spring creates EmployeeRepository.
Spring creates EmployeeService.
Spring manages their lifecycle.

This is Inversion of Control.
#What is Dependency Injection (DI)?
-------------------------------------

Definition:
Dependency Injection is the way Spring implements IoC. Instead of an object creating its dependencies, Spring injects them.

In the example:

@Autowired
private EmployeeRepository repository;

Spring injects the EmployeeRepository object into EmployeeService.

Real-Life Analogy

Imagine you buy a car.

Without DI

You buy the car and install the engine yourself.

Car
 |
 ---> Creates Engine
With DI

The manufacturer provides the car with the engine already installed.

Spring Container
      |
Creates Engine
      |
Injects into Car

The car simply uses the engine.

Types of Dependency Injection
1. Constructor Injection (Recommended)
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    private final EmployeeRepository repository;

    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Advantages:

Best practice
Makes dependencies mandatory
Easy to unit test
Works well with immutable fields
2. Setter Injection
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Used when the dependency is optional or may change.

3. Field Injection
@Autowired
private EmployeeRepository repository;

Simple, but generally not recommended because it makes testing harder and hides dependencies.

#What is Bean Lifecycle?
-------------------------

A Spring Bean Lifecycle is the sequence of steps a bean goes through from creation to destruction inside the Spring IoC Container.

Bean Lifecycle Steps
1. Bean Instantiation

Spring creates the bean.

@Component
public class EmployeeService {

    public EmployeeService() {
        System.out.println("Bean Created");
    }
}
2. Dependency Injection

Spring injects required dependencies.

@Autowired
private EmployeeRepository repository;
3. Initialization

Spring calls initialization methods after all dependencies are injected.

Using @PostConstruct
import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;

@Component
public class EmployeeService {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Initialization");
    }
}

This method is called once, after dependency injection.

4. Bean Ready

Now the bean is available for use.

employeeService.saveEmployee();
5. Destruction

When the application shuts down, Spring calls cleanup methods.

Using @PreDestroy
import jakarta.annotation.PreDestroy;

@Component
public class EmployeeService {

    @PreDestroy
    public void destroy() {
        System.out.println("Bean Destroyed");
    }
}

This is typically used to:

Close database connections
Close files
Release resources
Stop background threads

